---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Grid from '../../components/Grid.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/CallToAction.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Before a Storm"
  description="Preparedness workshops, emergency readiness kits, and neighborhood weather stations from the Florida Lantern Project."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Before a storm"
    title="The cheapest help is the help that arrives early"
    lede="Most of what makes a hurricane survivable is decided weeks before landfall — whether you have a plan, whether you have documentation, whether anyone nearby knows you're there."
  />

  <Section>
    <Grid>
      <Card title="Preparedness workshops" meta="Free">
        <p>
          Practical emergency planning for families and community groups: what to
          document, what to pack, when to leave, and who to tell.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Emergency readiness kits" meta="Sliding scale">
        <p>
          Assembled kits for families and businesses. Free for low-income
          households affected by disaster; sliding-scale pricing otherwise.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Weather stations" meta="Sliding scale">
        <p>
          We install weather stations and webcams that feed open-source weather
          networks, improving hyper-local forecasting for the whole neighborhood —
          not just the property they sit on.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Pre-storm property inspection" meta="Sliding scale">
        <p>
          A drone survey and written report of your property <em>before</em> a
          storm. When you file a claim afterwards, you have documented proof of
          what the roof looked like the week before.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </Grid>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="surface" title="Why pre-storm documentation matters">
    <p>
      After Milton, the families who struggled most weren't always the ones with
      the worst damage. They were the ones who couldn't prove what the damage
      replaced. Insurers ask for before-and-after. Most people only have after.
    </p>
    <p>
      A pre-storm inspection is thirty minutes and a PDF. It is the single
      cheapest thing you can do to protect a claim.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <CallToAction title="Want a workshop in your neighborhood?" href="/contact" cta="Get in touch">
    We run preparedness sessions for schools, churches, HOAs, and community
    groups across Tampa Bay.
  </CallToAction>
</BaseLayout>
